Well defined virtual filesystem representation

The current representation is poorly defined. This task tracks re-implementing the
virtual filesystem within git for tsk to be well defined.

Each task is a folder represented as a single tree object in the git object store.
Within that tree are files for the properties and content of the task. The content
itself is a property, but is typically filtered from property queries. The title may be
cached as a separate file called `title`, but it is optional, as the canonical title
should always be the first line of the `content`. Tasks do *not* have IDs at this
layer, IDs are instead the folder name of the tree object one layer above. The
`assigned` property is a back-reference to the queue the task is assigned to.

Modifications to a task are shared across all namespaces with each task having a
distinct linear commit history. Tasks are assigned a stable ID upon creation that is
shared across all views of that commit and implemented as a ref pointing to the latest
commit on the task. The format of these IDs does not need to be human readable, but it
should generally be terse. The SHA hash of the initial value is one possible
representation of these. Commits are automatically generated detailing the modification
made to the tasks tree, such as "updated content", "add property", "removed property",
"updated property value", etc. (these are not necessary the exact messages that should
be used).

A "namespace" is a collection of zero or more task commit histories unified into a
single view and represented as a tree with a particular structure. Human readable
identifiers are mapped to the task tree objects (very similar to how submodules are
represented). The namespace contains most of the things it does now: the `next` value
and the set of tasks. Namespaces are basically the same as branches, except they make
special care to avoid modifications to the task "submodules" within them from affecting
their history, their primary purpose is to provide a human readable name and a logical
view over the task set. Note: priorities are not handled here. Most repositories will
only contain a single namespace. The default namespace is "tsk".

Internal links using the [[tsk-<id>]] syntax are resolved on write to their stable
identifier, and resolved back to a human readable ID on read (or ignored if the
identifier is unknown). Namespaces are self-contained and are not intended to reference
tasks in other namespaces directly.

Queues are the relative representation for priority of tasks. Tasks are generally
expected to exist in only one queue. Queues are represented as tree objects, with an
`index` blob containing the ordered list of stable identifiers of tasks in the queue and
an `inbox` where tasks that are to be assigned to the queue sit. Queues are pointed to
by human-readable names using refs. Only one queue may be active at a time. Newly
created tasks follow the push/append semantics on the index of the currently active
queue. Assigning tasks to another queue removes its ref from the current queues index
and puts it in the inbox of the destination queue. Tasks that are accepted from the
inbox are moved into the index of the currently active queue, to the top by default but
controllable with flags to the accept command. Tasks may be rejected and reassigned to
the source queue. Queues are represented as commits over their tree objects, but care
must be taken to keep the history short, as the only reason to have them be
represented as commits instead of refs pointing to a tree is to allow for conflict
resolution (I'm open to more ideas here though).

The `tsk` command handles automatically generating commits and pushing/pulling refs in
most cases. Possible properties are indexed using a tree object pointed to by the
properties ref. How these indices are represented must be robust to conflicts, so they
likely need to be their own commit histories as well.